wa_accendo_lunae_lib_west_face_var beta
  - P1-P4 and most of P5 are the standard Let It Burn line, then two pitches (5.12- and 5.11-) lead into the West Face finish.
  + The standard Let It Burn line covers P1-P4 and most of P5; two further pitches (5.12- and 5.11-) then lead into the West Face finish.
  - P2 (5.10+): another short splitter, walk left to a second tree belay. P3 (5.12-): a few 5.10 cracks past a 5.11 slot where the old fixed pin has been removed, leaving a good cam placement; crux is two bolts moving left; belay at a two-bolt anchor.
  + P2 (5.10+): another brief splitter; shift left to a second belay at a tree. P3 (5.12-): a few 5.10 cracks go past a 5.11 slot, where the old fixed pin has been removed and a good cam placement remains; the crux is two bolts while moving left; belay at a two-bolt anchor.
  - P4 (5.11a): hands and fingers crack to knobs and a double crack; belay at a fixed pin and bolt.
  + P4 (5.11a): a crack in hands and fingers leads to knobs and then a double crack; belay at a bolt and a fixed pin.
  - P5 (5.11+): flare crack becoming a lieback; belay on 1" gear on a rightward bench. P6 (5.12-): a difficult flake/crack sequence; belay in the chimney right of a razor flake. P7 (5.11-): tips V-slot to a roof, then a corner.
  + P5 (5.11+): a flaring crack turns into a lieback; belay on 1" gear at a bench that trends right. P6 (5.12-): a hard flake/crack sequence; belay in the chimney to the right of a razor flake. P7 (5.11-): go up a tips V-slot, reaching a roof, then a corner.
  - P9 (5.10): overhanging chimneys to a large ledge. P10 (5.6): easy 300 feet to the summit.
  + P9 (5.10): chimneys that overhang lead to a big ledge. P10 (5.6): 300 feet of easy ground to the summit.

wa_accendo_lunae_lib_west_face_var pitch_detail[3].notes
  - Hands and fingers on knobs, fins and thin cracks, stepping right twice into new crack systems, to a double crack. Belay at a fixed pin and a bolt on a small ledge.
  + Work hands and fingers up knobs, fins and thin cracks, with two steps right into fresh crack systems, finishing at a double crack. Belay on a small ledge from a fixed pin plus a bolt.

wa_accendo_lunae_lib_west_face_var pitch_detail[5].notes
  - Traverse right, then climb a gently overhanging line of thin cracks and tips locks. The crux passes a wedged flake that flexes, so gear placements beside it are unreliable.
  + Traverse right, then follow a gently overhanging line of thin cracks and tips locks. A wedged flake that flexes marks the crux, so gear placed beside it can't be trusted.
  - Belay in a small alcove in the chimney just right of a razor-sharp flake.
  + Belay in a small alcove inside the chimney, immediately to the right of a razor-sharp flake.

wa_accendo_lunae_lib_west_face_var pitch_detail[6].notes
  - Awkward body positions up a tips crack in a V-shaped flare lead to a stance under a 4 ft roof where the corner switches from right-facing to left-facing.
  + Awkward body positions up a tips crack in a V-shaped flare lead to a stance under a 4 ft roof, at which the corner changes from facing right to facing left.

